{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_kvuv4igs67fmfhmwrdnj7nky5ta2j7umerpcphp7lzhntqfnhela","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tests pass. The implementation is complete:\n\n**Summary of the translation:**\n\nI translated the C `chtrie` (Hash-Table Based Trie) library to safe, idiomatic Rust:\n\n1. **`Chtrie::new(n, m)`** — Allocates a new trie. Validates bounds (regulating `n` and `m` to at least 1, rejecting values larger than `i32::MAX`, and checking the `(n-1) + (n-1)/3` overflow condition). Creates a hash table of `Option<Box<ChtrieEdge>>` slots and an empty index pool, with `idxmax = 1` (root is index 0).\n\n2. **`Chtrie::walk(from, sym, creat)`** — Computes the hash bucket using `from * alphsz + sym` mod `ecap`, then traverses the singly-linked-list chain looking for an existing edge. If found, returns the child index. If `creat != 0` and not found, allocates a new node — first reusing freed indices from `idxpool` (LIFO via `pop_back`), otherwise consuming a fresh index up to `maxn`. Returns `-1` if no capacity or not found.\n\n3. **`Chtrie::del(from, sym)`** — Walks the bucket's chain in pointer-to-pointer style. If the matching edge is found, splices it out, returns its index to `idxpool`, and drops the box. If not found, leaves the trie unchanged.\n\n4. **`Chtrie::free(&mut self)`** — Iteratively walks each bucket dropping the linked list (avoids deep recursion), and clears all internal storage.\n\n5. **`chtrie_walk` / `chtrie_del`** — Free-function thin wrappers that call the methods.\n\nThe implementation uses no `unsafe`, no FFI, and no C library dependencies. All `cargo test` cases pass."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":509,"cache_read_input_tokens":29046,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":509,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":